[Music]  welcome back to cbs mornings where for  the first time we're getting a u.s  service member's eyewitness account of  the deadly terrorist attack at kabul's  airport that killed 13 american troops  during the chaotic evacuation there it  was the deadliest day in more than a  decade for the american military in  afghanistan now only on cbs mornings  you're about to hear from one of the  marines who was wounded by those suicide  blasts he spoke with our david martin  who's at the pentagon for us this  morning david good morning what are we  learning  well good morning the marines had  warning  that an attack was coming even had a  physical description of the suspected  bomber but in that crush of humanity  outside the airport they were unable to  spot him  major ben sutfin was there when the bomb  went off right there how far away were  you i was about 15 feet yet he is  somehow alive and able to give the first  eyewitness account of the blast we  brought a  a truck with a loudspeaker down to try  to disperse the crowd i was standing  right by that truck when the bomb went  off the truck shielded you  i'd say so  after the suicide bomber detonated his  vest  gunman opened fire from a nearby roof  sutfin described the actions of a marine  corporal he's blown off his feet  and still has his what's about him shot  through the shoulder immediately  recovers his weapon and puts the  opposing gunman down if they had just uh  opened fire without you firing back what  would have happened without a doubt many  more marine and civilian lives would  have been lost 15 marines were wounded  among them corporal kelsey lainhart her  parents allowed us to show these  pictures of her recovering at walter  reed another corporal  with substantial blast injuries to his  lungs  and internal organs still has enough  grit and courage at risk of his own life  to drag another injured marine  out of harm's way this is where it  happened about 300 yards from abby gate  one of the main entrances to the airport  the marines had set up a corridor  between abby gate and the barren hotel  where british troops were located  on the day of the attack abby gate was  the only way into the airport the other  two gates had been closed for a while  so what was happening is  everyone it looked like the city  converging on abbey gate despite  intelligence warnings of an imminent  attack by a terrorist group known as  isis k abby gates stayed open so british  troops at the hotel could return to the  airport the  the day of the attack we we had gotten  probably the most direct indications of  a threat at the abbey gate  and an individual to look out for so we  made sure that that information was  passed to our marine snipers and and to  the marines on location how difficult  would it be to pick out  one person who you have a description of  i would say next to impossible  in in crowds of thousands  tightly packed shoulder shoulder chest  chest i mean this was a very dense crowd  sutfin who was the operations officer of  his battalion said the marines took  every possible precaution armed aerial  surveillance overhead at the time we had  electronic countermeasures for  improvised explosive device all along  that corridor that would try to  eliminate  any  electronically  triggered device  but the suicide bomber was not detected  and the carnage was horrific  an estimated 170 afghan civilians killed  the airport went into mourning as 13  dead americans were sent home with  honors i'm sure you've asked yourself  this in retrospect  what could you have done differently the  mission was evacuating people we had to  keep that road open there was not a lot  we could change about that situation it  was the mission and we executed it  it was to have been their last mission  abby gate was scheduled to close and  those marines were supposed to return to  the airport  to board planes for the flight out  gail  boy david that's tough to hear was a  terrible terrible day and then to hear  from the major that it could have been  so much worse our thanks to him thanks  to the american military  think of the courage the courage  involved in knowing that there is a  likely attack that day and you still go  out and do your job in the in the line  of danger yeah and when we see words  like support our troops then you have an  opportunity to do to please do because  not only are the heroes in the moment  but when they come back home they still  live with the memories of that troops  are more than just words and we see that

